.TH MU2E-PROBE 1 "2026-09-18" "mu2edaq-power-recovery 0.1.0" "Mu2e DAQ Operations"
.SH NAME
mu2e-probe \- parallel TCP reachability sweep of DAQ hosts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mu2e-probe
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ host ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Probes many hosts in parallel and reports which ones answer. With no host
arguments, hosts are read from standard input, one per line, so it composes
with
.BR mu2e-node-inventory (1).
.PP
The probe is a non-blocking TCP connect, not ICMP. It needs no privilege (a raw
ICMP socket needs root), it answers a more useful question than "the IP stack
is up", and TCP/22 is not filtered on the lab network because the cluster is
administered over it.
.PP
A
.I refused
connection counts as reachable: the machine answered, its sshd simply is not
listening yet. During a power-on that is the normal intermediate state, and
treating it as "down" would report most of the cluster as dead.
.PP
This is the command-line face of
.BR libmu2eprobe (3),
which is also what
.BR mu2e-power-recovery (1)
uses to choose a responsive gateway quickly. The library is optional; when it
is not built, the Python tools fall back to their own implementation and this
command is simply absent. It is not a Python console script: build it with
.RS
.EX
\&./bootstrap.sh \-\-with\-cpp
.EE
.RE
which leaves the binary at
.IR build/mu2e-probe .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-p " PORT" "\fR, \fP" \-\-port " PORT"
TCP port to probe. Default: 22. 623 reaches a BMC, but note the limitation: IPMI
RMCP+ is a UDP protocol, and this command only ever opens a TCP connection, so
a sweep of port 623 says whether the BMC has a TCP listener there \(em not
whether it will answer an IPMI session. For that question use
.BR mu2e-ipmi-tool (1)
with
.BR "chassis power status" .
.TP
.BI \-t " MS" "\fR, \fP" \-\-timeout " MS"
Per-host connect budget in milliseconds. Default: 2000.
.TP
.BI \-j " N" "\fR, \fP" \-\-threads " N"
Worker threads. Default: the hardware thread count, via OpenMP. In a build
without OpenMP the sweep is serial and this option is ignored;
.B \-\-version
says which build this is.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-resolve\-only
Resolve names and stop; do not connect.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-up\-only
Print only the hosts that answered.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Print nothing; use the exit status.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the library version, and whether it was built with OpenMP.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage summary and exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
This command is a C++ binary and reads none of the project's configuration: no
YAML file, no
.IR .env ,
no
.B MU2E_POWER_RECOVERY_*
variable. It takes its hosts from the command line or standard input and
nothing else, and it does not know the topology, so pipe
.BR mu2e-node-inventory (1)
into it when you want a named set of hosts.
.PP
.B OMP_NUM_THREADS
is honoured in an OpenMP build, because without
.B \-j
the worker count is left to OpenMP;
.B \-j
overrides it. Neither has any effect in a serial build.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every host answered.
.TP
.B 1
At least one host did not.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.EX
mu2e-probe mu2e-trk-01 mu2e-trk-02
mu2e-node-inventory \-\-hostnames | mu2e-probe \-\-timeout 1000
mu2e-node-inventory \-n ipmi \-\-hostnames | mu2e-probe \-\-port 623 \-u
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR libmu2eprobe (3),
.BR mu2e-node-inventory (1),
.BR mu2e-power-recovery (1)
.SH AUTHOR
A. Norman <anorman@fnal.gov>, Mu2e DAQ Operations.
